VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-025
Reason for Visit: Progressive, severe swelling and heaviness in the right leg with a new area of localized redness and pain.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported chronic massive limb swelling complicated by acute localized pain and redness. This presentation raises a high suspicion for acute cellulitis supervening on chronic lymphedema. Urgent assessment was strictly required to differentiate this from a deep vein thrombosis, evaluate for systemic sepsis, and initiate immediate antibiotic therapy to prevent rapid spread in a compromised lymphatic limb, precluding safe delay until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My right leg has been huge and heavy for years, but today a patch of skin turned bright red, hot, and extremely painful."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 5-year history of chronic progressive unilateral swelling of the right lower extremity following pelvic surgery. Describes a constant heaviness and dull aching sensation in the affected limb. The swelling is typically non-pitting. Over the last 24 hours, the patient developed a new, localized area of intense erythema, warmth, and pain on the right shin. Reports a low-grade fever but denies chills or systemic toxicity. KEY SYMPTOMS: Chronic progressive unilateral swelling of the extremities. Heaviness or a dull aching sensation in the affected limb. Recurrent localized episodes of cellulitis. Complete absence of prominent varicose veins or dependent rubor.
Functional Status: Mobility is significantly restricted due to the severe swelling and weight of the limb.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; mobility limitations increase the risk of falls and isolation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98 percent | Wt: 95 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, shifting weight off the right leg, mildly febrile.
Physical Exam: Extremities: Right lower extremity exhibits massive, asymmetric, non-pitting edema extending from the toes to the groin. The skin is thickened, hyperkeratotic, and fibrotic with a 'peau d'orange' appearance. Positive Stemmer's sign (inability to pinch a fold of skin at the base of the second right toe). Superimposed on the mid-shin is a 10cm x 8cm sharply demarcated area of bright erythema, intense warmth, and exquisite tenderness. Complete absence of prominent varicose veins. Cardiovascular: Normal peripheral pulses palpable (with difficulty) via Doppler. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Chronic non-pitting extremity swelling, Positive Stemmer's sign, Hyperkeratotic skin changes, Delayed transit on lymphoscintigraphy (assumed from chronic history).
Environmental Observations: Compression garments laid out but currently not worn.
Medication Reconciliation: No current antibiotics. Previously treated for similar skin infections.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Lymphedema complicated by Acute Cellulitis - Status: Exacerbated (Infected)
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The swelling is chronic (>5 years), non-pitting, and hyperkeratotic, and the acute change is a localized, sharply demarcated, hot, erythematous plaque typical of superficial infection rather than a deep, diffuse calf ache and swelling.

Chronic Venous Insufficiency - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of varicose veins, hemosiderin staining, and venous ulceration; Stemmer's sign is distinctively positive for lymphatic etiology.

Acute Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of fever, localized intense warmth, and exquisite tenderness, which point to bacterial infection rather than an allergic reaction.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits textbook signs of chronic lymphedema (massive non-pitting edema, hyperkeratosis, positive Stemmer's sign) that is now acutely complicated by superficial cellulitis. Lymphatic-compromised limbs are highly susceptible to rapid, severe bacterial infections. The acute onset of fever, expanding erythema, and localized pain warranted an urgent after-hours physical assessment to confirm the diagnosis, definitively rule out DVT, and immediately initiate oral antibiotics. Delaying treatment until routine hours posed an unacceptable risk of the infection progressing to systemic sepsis or requiring intravenous therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-dose oral antibiotics (e.g., Flucloxacillin 500mg QID for 10 days) to aggressively treat the cellulitis. Marked the leading edge of the erythema with a pen to monitor for spread.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to practice meticulous skin hygiene, washing the leg daily with a pH-neutral soap and applying a thick emollient, while avoiding all scratching. By actively maintaining the skin barrier, the patient physically blocks bacterial entry points. The reward is a drastic reduction in the frequency of painful, debilitating cellulitis flares.
Home-Based Services: Referred to community nursing for regular lower limb wound/skin monitoring.
DME/Safety: Advised to refrain from wearing tight compression garments over the acutely inflamed area until the pain and redness subside.
Follow-up: General Practice review in 48 hours is mandatory to ensure the leading edge of the infection is retreating.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness rapidly spreads beyond the pen marks, if the patient develops a high spiking fever with uncontrollable shivering (rigors), or experiences severe nausea/vomiting, they must present to the Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of cellulitis is clinical; underlying lymphatic transit issues require specialist imaging.
Clinical Course: Cellulitis in a lymphedematous limb can progress rapidly. Strict adherence to the antibiotic course is mandatory, even if the redness begins to fade early.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Take the antibiotics exactly on time. Keep the leg elevated. Seek immediate emergency care if the redness spreads quickly or you develop severe shivering.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The chronic swelling in your leg has stretched and damaged the skin, allowing bacteria to enter and cause a rapid infection (cellulitis). We must treat this aggressively with strong antibiotics to clear the infection before it spreads into your bloodstream.
Actionable Steps: Take your antibiotics four times a day without fail. Keep your leg elevated above your hip when sitting to reduce the fluid pressure. Watch the pen marks daily.

